{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786507315,"nonce":"+qRLrgSD38xRH+Vn5dA58f5c+tM=","title":"**What is wrong.** CONTEXT.md §4 carries a module table and a dependency-direction diagram with nine arrows, and states that the direction is held by *\"depguard + an arch test\"*. Two of the nine arrows are import edges as drawn, one is implemented in the opposite direction, five real cross-module edges are absent from the diagram, two whole modules are absent from it, and **there is no arch test**.","message":"The diagram is not wrong about intent. It is wrong about being enforced, and it draws three unrelated mechanisms as one kind of arrow.\n\n**The demonstration.** The nine declared edges are `ingestion → alerts`, `alerts → grouping`, `grouping → notification`, `notification → channels`, `alerts → enrichment`, `grouping → streaming`, `notification → silences`, `enrichment → rules`, `rules → sources`.\n\nOf those, only `enrichment → rules` and `rules → sources` are service-level Go imports. `notification → channels` exists only as an import of `channels/domain`; `channels/service` is injected. `ingestion → alerts`, `grouping → streaming` and `notification → silences` are ports the consumer declares and `internal/app/container.go` satisfies. `grouping → notification` and `alerts → enrichment` are not calls at all — they are River job enqueues by kind (`notify.evaluate`, `enrich.run`), so the coupling is a string in `internal/platform/jobs/kinds.go`.\n\n`alerts → grouping` is **backwards**. `grouping/service` and `grouping/api` import `alerts/service`, not the reverse, and the crossing happens in `internal/app/adapters.go:893` — an adapter whose own comment calls it *\"THE INGEST ORCHESTRATOR: the one place that may know both `alerts` and `grouping`\"*.\n\nFive real edges are undrawn: `sources → alerts/service` (the reconciler), `silences → alerts/service`, `rules → alerts/service`, `identity → alerts/service`, and `enrichment → sources/client`. `internal/drill` and `internal/app` do not appear in the diagram at all — `drill` because it reaches its neighbours by writing their table names into SQL rather than importing them (see the `drill` ticket), `app` because it is the composition root. `internal/platform`'s absence is correct and deliberate: it is infrastructure, and `platform-must-not-import-domains` enforces that.\n\n`.golangci.yml` does not enforce direction. Its cross-domain rules are symmetric — every `\u003cx\u003e-must-not-reach-into-other-domains` rule re-allows *every* other `\u003cy\u003e/service`, so they constrain layering, not direction. The single directional rule is `dependency-direction-alerts-and-ingestion` (alerts and ingestion may not reach `notification` or `channels`). Tests are excluded entirely (`.golangci.yml:85`). A repo-wide search for an arch or import-graph test finds none; the nearest gates are `tools/lintreach` and `tools/lintvocab`, which check reachability and vocabulary, not module direction.\n\nSeparately, §4's own prose is stale: it credits `ingestion` with *\"run[ning] the reconciler\"*. It does not. `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:24-30` says so explicitly — SPEC §I.2 draws the reconciler as `internal/ingestion/worker/reconcile_source` and *\"It is implemented here instead.\"*\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the same failure this repo has closed several times — a document describing enforcement that does not run — and it is the most load-bearing instance, because §4 is what a new contributor or an agent reads to decide whether an import is allowed. A reader who trusts it will believe `alerts` may call `grouping` (it may not), that the arrows mark compile-time dependencies (mostly they do not), and that a violation would be caught (it would not). Drawing an injected port, a job enqueue and an import as the same arrow also hides the thing most worth knowing: only one of the three is checked by anything.\n\n**Where.** `CONTEXT.md` §4 (the module table and the diagram). `.golangci.yml` (the depguard block; `:85` excludes tests). `internal/app/adapters.go:893` (`alertObserver`, the reversed crossing). `internal/app/container.go` (where the injected ports are satisfied). `internal/platform/jobs/kinds.go` (the enqueue-by-string coupling). `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:24-30` (the reconciler's real home).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** §4 distinguishes the three kinds of edge it currently conflates, its arrows match the code's actual direction, `drill` and `app` appear with their real edges, the reconciler sentence is corrected — and either an arch test exists that fails on a violating import, or §4 stops claiming one and says which rules depguard actually enforces.","files":null}]}